From 4dc220be5e11847ad4778e3b3e35ae7a4411a0bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 22:50:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] PSCTRL: a .cfg writer that keeps the file, and a floppy
 TOS notices

config_file_save() is a LINE EDITOR over the original, not a serialiser.
The parser throws away comments, ordering and the spelling of every value
it reads - a bare `ttram` becomes 128M, fps is clamped, stram_size becomes
bytes - so dumping struct emulator_config back out would quietly rewrite
the file's meaning as well as strip its comments. Every line the emulator
does not manage is copied through byte for byte, a managed key that has a
line has that line replaced in place, and a managed key with no line is
appended once under a marked heading. The previous file is kept as .bak
and the new one is written to .tmp and renamed.

The key list is the descriptor table itself: load_config_file() hands any
key it does not recognise to psctrl_settings_config_key(), which matches
it against the same names, so a tunable added there needs no parser case.
The twelve boolean trace flags collapse onto one `debug mfp,ipl,acsi`
line - which answers the open question in the design doc.

Floppy: fdd_query() reports a drive to anything outside atari_fdd.c,
where everything interesting is behind a file-static (fdd_status() is
exported but every fprintf in it is commented out, so there was no way to
ask what is in drive A:).

And media change, finally. The ST has no disk-change line: TOS decides a
disk was swapped when flopvbl sees the write-protect state TOGGLE, and
only then does GEMDOS Mediach() answer 2 instead of serving the old
disk's cached FAT - which is what corrupts an image on the first write
after a swap. media_changed has been in fdd_drive_t since the start, set
by insert and eject and read by NOTHING. This is that reader: the first
Type I status read after a swap reports the WP bit inverted and clears
the flag.

UNVERIFIED ON HARDWARE, and the one part of this work that cannot be
reasoned to confidence from source: TOS 1.02 / 1.62 / 2.06 / EmuTOS all
differ here. The accessory also calls Getbpb() and walks the root after a
swap, which is the belt to this braces.
